{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CihyWyUszTsG3NtJrG7qqJ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — acr-vla-conformal-refinement\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: CoRL 2026 (abstract May 26, paper May 29) — 极紧，需 GPU 扩容至 ≥2x H100 才可行\n5\t- Backup: ICLR 2027 (expected deadline ~Sep-Oct 2026) — 充足时间\n6\t- 决策点: 若 3 天内 GPU 未扩容，放弃 CoRL，瞄 ICLR 2027\n7\t\n8\t## Core Insight\n9\tPer-DOF conformal statistics from multivariate CP on flow-matching VLA samples encode \"which action dimensions are uncertain and how they co-vary\" — fundamentally richer information than scalar uncertainty scores. A lightweight learned module conditioned on these structured features can perform dimension-selective action correction: aggressively correcting high-uncertainty DOFs while preserving confident predictions elsewhere.\n10\t\n11\t## Core Claims (3 条，均可证伪)\n12\t1. **Per-DOF conformal features enable dimension-selective correction**: CGS 提取的 45D 结构化 uncertainty 特征（per-DOF std + conformal radius + ellipsoid volume）比标量 uncertainty（ReconVLA 的 Euclidean distance）和无 uncertainty conditioning 的残差策略（A2C2 类）提供显著更好的 action correction。\n13\t   - 验证: MVP (✅ done) + A3 ablation (make-or-break) + A4 ablation + feature importance analysis\n14\t   - Kill criterion: A3 (CRM without conformal features) 与 full ACR-VLA 差距 <2% → claim 不成立\n15\t\n16\t2. **ACI+COC 在分布偏移下维持 calibrated uncertainty，无需外部监督**: Chunk Overlap Consistency 提供自参考覆盖信号，ACI 在线更新 conformal threshold，在任意分布偏移下维持长期平均覆盖保证。\n17\t   - 验证: COC-success Spearman ρ + ACI 收敛 + ellipsoid volume vs static\n18\t   - Kill criterion: Spearman ρ < 0.3 AND ellipsoid volume 无 >10% 收窄 → fallback to episode-level ACI\n19\t\n20\t3. **ACR-VLA 与 training-time 方法正交互补**: 作为 inference-time 模块可叠加在 SRPO (82.1% LIBERO-Plus SOTA) 上进一步提升。\n21\t   - 验证: ACR-VLA + SRPO > SRPO alone\n22\t   - Kill criterion: stacking gain < 2% → claim 降级为\"complementary in principle\"\n23\t\n24\t## Story Spine\n25\t- **Problem**: Flow-matching VLAs 在标准 benchmark 上接近饱和(97-99%)，但 LIBERO-Plus 暴露了分布偏移下的灾难性鲁棒性缺口。现有方法分别解决检测(SAFE)、样本选择(ReconVLA)、训练时增强(RobustVLA/DiG-Flow/SRPO)，但没有方法在推理时利用结构化 uncertainty 信息主动校正动作。\n26\t- **Gap**: ReconVLA 用标量 Euclidean distance 做样本选择——丢弃跨维度协方差结构，且无法合成优于任何单个样本的动作。CP-in-the-Loop 通过 planning 闭环但非学习型参数化。UPS 用 CP 做策略门控但不将 conformal 特征作为校正网络输入。A2C2/PLD 学习残差但不利用 calibrated uncertainty features。\n27\t- **Our angle**: 第一个将多元 conformal 统计量作为学习型校正模块直接输入特征的方法。关键 insight 是 per-DOF conformal 特征编码\"哪些维度不确定+跨维度协方差\"，使 CRM 能做维度选择性校正。\n28\t- **Headline result template**: \"On LIBERO-Plus, ACR-VLA improves base VLA success by X%, and when stacked on SRPO (82.1%), achieves Y% — demonstrating that inference-time conformal correction provides orthogonal value beyond training-time optimization.\"\n29\t\n30\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n31\t1. **K=10 covariance 估计可靠性** (p/n=0.7): Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage 是标准方案，MVP 已验证 K=10 estimated 仍有效（衰减 ~15pp 但仍显著）。若审稿人追问，可加 K=20 ablation。\n32\t2. **CRM 131K 参数表达力**: 故意轻量——防 overfitting + 保持推理速度。ablation 可测 dim 512 版本。\n33\t3. **Synthetic augmentation 覆盖不全** (layout/language 不可覆盖): Honest limitation。ACI 在线适应部分弥补。per-perturbation breakdown 是透明呈现。\n34\t4. **仿真 only**: CoRL 接受 sim-only，LIBERO-Plus 是 recognized benchmark。\n35\t5. **DiU 的 single-pass uncertainty 更高效**: DiU 捕获 aleatoric uncertainty（在分布内也高），CGS 捕获 epistemic uncertainty（分布偏移时升高）。A7 ablation 验证区分。\n36\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"674be60e-e4c3-45c8-be6d-42aa229c3df7","uuid":"29858d22-5f08-4735-82ad-bf4289e7b3ad","timestamp":"2026-05-13T02:57:55.615Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_8/projects/acr-vla-conformal-refinement/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T023849/median/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — acr-vla-conformal-refinement\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: CoRL 2026 (abstract May 26, paper May 29) — 极紧，需 GPU 扩容至 ≥2x H100 才可行\n- Backup: ICLR 2027 (expected deadline ~Sep-Oct 2026) — 充足时间\n- 决策点: 若 3 天内 GPU 未扩容，放弃 CoRL，瞄 ICLR 2027\n\n## Core Insight\nPer-DOF conformal statistics from multivariate CP on flow-matching VLA samples encode \"which action dimensions are uncertain and how they co-vary\" — fundamentally richer information than scalar uncertainty scores. A lightweight learned module conditioned on these structured features can perform dimension-selective action correction: aggressively correcting high-uncertainty DOFs while preserving confident predictions elsewhere.\n\n## Core Claims (3 条，均可证伪)\n1. **Per-DOF conformal features enable dimension-selective correction**: CGS 提取的 45D 结构化 uncertainty 特征（per-DOF std + conformal radius + ellipsoid volume）比标量 uncertainty（ReconVLA 的 Euclidean distance）和无 uncertainty conditioning 的残差策略（A2C2 类）提供显著更好的 action correction。\n   - 验证: MVP (✅ done) + A3 ablation (make-or-break) + A4 ablation + feature importance analysis\n   - Kill criterion: A3 (CRM without conformal features) 与 full ACR-VLA 差距 <2% → claim 不成立\n\n2. **ACI+COC 在分布偏移下维持 calibrated uncertainty，无需外部监督**: Chunk Overlap Consistency 提供自参考覆盖信号，ACI 在线更新 conformal threshold，在任意分布偏移下维持长期平均覆盖保证。\n   - 验证: COC-success Spearman ρ + ACI 收敛 + ellipsoid volume vs static\n   - Kill criterion: Spearman ρ < 0.3 AND ellipsoid volume 无 >10% 收窄 → fallback to episode-level ACI\n\n3. **ACR-VLA 与 training-time 方法正交互补**: 作为 inference-time 模块可叠加在 SRPO (82.1% LIBERO-Plus SOTA) 上进一步提升。\n   - 验证: ACR-VLA + SRPO > SRPO alone\n   - Kill criterion: stacking gain < 2% → claim 降级为\"complementary in principle\"\n\n## Story Spine\n- **Problem**: Flow-matching VLAs 在标准 benchmark 上接近饱和(97-99%)，但 LIBERO-Plus 暴露了分布偏移下的灾难性鲁棒性缺口。现有方法分别解决检测(SAFE)、样本选择(ReconVLA)、训练时增强(RobustVLA/DiG-Flow/SRPO)，但没有方法在推理时利用结构化 uncertainty 信息主动校正动作。\n- **Gap**: ReconVLA 用标量 Euclidean distance 做样本选择——丢弃跨维度协方差结构，且无法合成优于任何单个样本的动作。CP-in-the-Loop 通过 planning 闭环但非学习型参数化。UPS 用 CP 做策略门控但不将 conformal 特征作为校正网络输入。A2C2/PLD 学习残差但不利用 calibrated uncertainty features。\n- **Our angle**: 第一个将多元 conformal 统计量作为学习型校正模块直接输入特征的方法。关键 insight 是 per-DOF conformal 特征编码\"哪些维度不确定+跨维度协方差\"，使 CRM 能做维度选择性校正。\n- **Headline result template**: \"On LIBERO-Plus, ACR-VLA improves base VLA success by X%, and when stacked on SRPO (82.1%), achieves Y% — demonstrating that inference-time conformal correction provides orthogonal value beyond training-time optimization.\"\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n1. **K=10 covariance 估计可靠性** (p/n=0.7): Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage 是标准方案，MVP 已验证 K=10 estimated 仍有效（衰减 ~15pp 但仍显著）。若审稿人追问，可加 K=20 ablation。\n2. **CRM 131K 参数表达力**: 故意轻量——防 overfitting + 保持推理速度。ablation 可测 dim 512 版本。\n3. **Synthetic augmentation 覆盖不全** (layout/language 不可覆盖): Honest limitation。ACI 在线适应部分弥补。per-perturbation breakdown 是透明呈现。\n4. **仿真 only**: CoRL 接受 sim-only，LIBERO-Plus 是 recognized benchmark。\n5. **DiU 的 single-pass uncertainty 更高效**: DiU 捕获 aleatoric uncertainty（在分布内也高），CGS 捕获 epistemic uncertainty（分布偏移时升高）。A7 ablation 验证区分。\n","numLines":36,"startLine":1,"totalLines":36}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013urXbt3VhUEE7LEqSQAZxs","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"ACR-VLA Full Pipeline (SmolVLA base)\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Full ACR-VLA pipeline: CGS (K=10, Ledoit-Wolf) + ACI/COC + CRM (131K params). CRM trained on LIBERO demos + synthetic augmentation (camera jitter, Gaussian noise, robot-state perturbation). Evaluated on LIBERO nominal (10 tasks) and LIBERO-Plus (7 perturbation dimensions × 10 tasks).\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"libero_nominal_success_rate\": 92.6,\n7\t      \"libero_nominal_base\": 94.8,\n8\t      \"libero_nominal_drop\": 2.2,\n9\t      \"libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate\": 70.5,\n10\t      \"libero_plus_base\": 62.3,\n11\t      \"libero_plus_gain_abs\": 8.2,\n12\t      \"libero_plus_camera_shift\": 70.8,\n13\t      \"libero_plus_sensor_noise\": 66.4,\n14\t      \"libero_plus_robot_state\": 65.3,\n15\t      \"libero_plus_lighting\": 71.2,\n16\t      \"libero_plus_background\": 71.5,\n17\t      \"libero_plus_layout\": 73.0,\n18\t      \"libero_plus_language\": 72.4,\n19\t      \"crm_inference_overhead_ms\": 3.2,\n20\t      \"cgs_k10_sampling_time_ms\": 118,\n21\t      \"total_inference_time_per_step_ms\": 134\n22\t    },\n23\t    \"tables\": [\n24\t      {\n25\t        \"name\": \"Table 1: LIBERO and LIBERO-Plus Success Rates\",\n26\t        \"content\": \"| Method       | LIBERO (nominal) | LIBERO-Plus (agg.) | Δ Plus |\\n|-------------|------------------|-------------------|--------|\\n| SmolVLA     | 94.8             | 62.3              | —      |\\n| ACR-VLA     | 92.6             | 70.5              | +8.2   |\\n\"\n27\t      },\n28\t      {\n29\t        \"name\": \"Table 1 (extended): Per-Perturbation Breakdown\",\n30\t        \"content\": \"| Perturbation  | SmolVLA | ACR-VLA | Δ     | Significant? |\\n|--------------|---------|---------|-------|-------------|\\n| camera_shift | 58.2    | 70.8    | +12.6 | ✓           |\\n| sensor_noise | 55.6    | 66.4    | +10.8 | ✓           |\\n| robot_state  | 56.8    | 65.3    | +8.5  | ✓           |\\n| lighting     | 65.4    | 71.2    | +5.8  | ✓           |\\n| background   | 68.3    | 71.5    | +3.2  | ✗           |\\n| layout       | 72.1    | 73.0    | +0.9  | ✗           |\\n| language     | 71.5    | 72.4    | +0.9  | ✗           |\\n\"\n31\t      }\n32\t    ],\n33\t    \"figures\": [],\n34\t    \"analysis\": \"The 8.2% aggregate gain is solidly within the median range [6, 11]. The per-perturbation structure is informative: the 4 dimensions with significant gain (camera, noise, robot-state, lighting) are precisely those covered by synthetic augmentation, confirming that CGS calibration quality is the bottleneck. Layout and language perturbations involve semantic shifts that synthetic augmentation cannot cover — CGS features for these are essentially noise, and CRM correctly learns to make near-zero corrections. The 2.2% nominal drop is acceptable for CoRL but should be discussed honestly.\\n\\n\\nFlow-matching samples from real VLA have covariance structure but noisier than synthetic MVP: K=10 Ledoit-Wolf estimates are adequate but not tight, introducing ~15-20% feature quality degradation vs oracle covariance. CRM still learns meaningful correction patterns because per-DOF std and conformal radii carry genuine distributional information even with shrunk covariance. The 8.2% aggregate gain comes primarily from camera/noise/robot-state dimensions where synthetic augmentation provides good calibration-set coverage — CRM learns to suppress high-uncertainty DOFs (typically wrist rotation and z-translation under camera shift) while preserving confident DOFs. The 2.2% nominal drop is CRM occasionally over-correcting in-distribution actions where CGS correctly reports low uncertainty but CRM's learned residual still nudges slightly; this is the cost of the correction module's expressivity. Gain is real but not as dramatic as the MVP's ~27% per-DOF advantage because real VLA action distributions are higher-dimensional (7-DOF chunks vs 3D), multimodal, and the covariance structure is harder to estimate reliably with K=10.\\n\"\n35\t  },\n36\t  {\n37\t    \"name\": \"A3 Ablation — CRM without Conformal Features\",\n38\t    \"description\": \"CRM receives only observation embeddings (from VLA encoder) as input, no CGS-derived uncertainty features. Equivalent to an A2C2-style generic learned residual policy. Same CRM architecture (131K params), same training data and procedure as full ACR-VLA.\\n\",\n39\t    \"results\": {\n40\t      \"libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate\": 65.8,\n41\t      \"libero_plus_gain_over_base\": 3.5,\n42\t      \"a3_gap_to_full_acr_vla\": 4.7,\n43\t      \"libero_nominal_success_rate\": 93.8,\n44\t      \"libero_plus_camera_shift\": 63.1,\n45\t      \"libero_plus_sensor_noise\": 60.2,\n46\t      \"libero_plus_robot_state\": 60.9,\n47\t      \"libero_plus_lighting\": 67.8,\n48\t      \"libero_plus_background\": 69.5,\n49\t      \"libero_plus_layout\": 72.6,\n50\t      \"libero_plus_language\": 71.8\n51\t    },\n52\t    \"tables\": [\n53\t      {\n54\t        \"name\": \"Ablation Table Row (A3)\",\n55\t        \"content\": \"| Variant              | LIBERO-Plus | Δ vs Base | Δ vs Full ACR-VLA |\\n|---------------------|-------------|-----------|-------------------|\\n| SmolVLA (base)      | 62.3        | —         | -8.2              |\\n| A3: CRM, no unc.   | 65.8        | +3.5      | -4.7              |\\n| Full ACR-VLA        | 70.5        | +8.2      | —                 |\\n\"\n56\t      }\n57\t    ],\n58\t    \"figures\": [],\n59\t    \"analysis\": \"A3 gap of 4.7% clearly exceeds the 2% kill threshold. Claim 1 survives but the narrative must acknowledge that observation-conditioned residuals alone provide non-trivial value (+3.5%). The story should frame this as: \\\"CRM provides a correction mechanism; conformal features provide the information that makes the correction dimension-selective and magnitude-appropriate.\\\"\\n\\n\\nWithout uncertainty features, CRM can only learn a fixed residual correction pattern conditioned on visual observations. It captures some systematic biases (e.g., consistent z-offset under camera shift) but cannot adapt correction magnitude or direction to per-step uncertainty. The 3.5% gain over base reflects that observation-conditioned residuals capture low-hanging fruit — average correction vectors that are right more often than wrong. But the lack of dimension-selective information means CRM applies similar corrections to confident and uncertain DOFs alike, sometimes degrading already-accurate dimensions. The 4.7% gap to full ACR-VLA (within [3,7]) confirms that conformal features provide substantial value — almost as much as the residual module itself — but the gap isn't overwhelming, consistent with the median tier's \\\"per-DOF advantage exists but attenuated\\\" characterization. The kill criterion (gap < 2%) is clearly survived; Claim 1 holds with the softened framing.\\n\"\n60\t  },\n61\t  {\n62\t    \"name\": \"A4 Ablation — Scalar Uncertainty Features\",\n63\t    \"description\": \"CRM receives observation embeddings + scalar uncertainty features (Euclidean distance from mean to nearest calibration point, analogous to ReconVLA's approach) instead of 45D per-DOF features. Scalar features: 1D distance + 1D log-likelihood + 1D normalized rank = 3D total.\\n\",\n64\t    \"results\": {\n65\t      \"libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate\": 68.4,\n66\t      \"libero_plus_gain_over_base\": 6.1,\n67\t      \"a4_gap_to_full_acr_vla\": 2.1,\n68\t      \"a4_gap_to_a3\": 2.6,\n69\t      \"libero_nominal_success_rate\": 93.2,\n70\t      \"libero_plus_camera_shift\": 67.5,\n71\t      \"libero_plus_sensor_noise\": 63.8,\n72\t      \"libero_plus_robot_state\": 63.0,\n73\t      \"libero_plus_lighting\": 69.8,\n74\t      \"libero_plus_background\": 70.4,\n75\t      \"libero_plus_layout\": 72.8,\n76\t      \"libero_plus_language\": 72.1\n77\t    },\n78\t    \"tables\": [\n79\t      {\n80\t        \"name\": \"Ablation Table Row (A4)\",\n81\t        \"content\": \"| Variant              | LIBERO-Plus | Δ vs Base | Δ vs A3 | Δ vs Full |\\n|---------------------|-------------|-----------|---------|-----------|\\n| SmolVLA (base)      | 62.3        | —         | —       | -8.2      |\\n| A3: CRM, no unc.   | 65.8        | +3.5      | —       | -4.7      |\\n| A4: CRM + scalar   | 68.4        | +6.1      | +2.6    | -2.1      |\\n| Full ACR-VLA        | 70.5        | +8.2      | +4.7    | —         |\\n\"\n82\t      }\n83\t    ],\n84\t    \"figures\": [],\n85\t    \"analysis\": \"The A4 gap of 2.1% is within [1,3] as expected for median tier. The critical insight for the paper: the decomposition base→A3→A4→Full shows gains of +3.5/+2.6/+2.1. The transition from \\\"no uncertainty\\\" to \\\"any uncertainty\\\" (+2.6) is larger than \\\"scalar to per-DOF\\\" (+2.1), but per-DOF still provides meaningful additional value. The narrative should emphasize that all three components contribute and per-DOF is the final piece that enables truly dimension-selective correction.\\n\\n\\nScalar uncertainty gives CRM a \\\"how uncertain overall\\\" signal, enabling it to modulate correction magnitude globally — correct more aggressively when scalar uncertainty is high, less when low. This captures the bulk of the uncertainty-conditioning benefit (+2.6% over A3), because the dominant failure mode under distribution shift is \\\"everything is somewhat off\\\" rather than \\\"one specific DOF is wrong.\\\" The remaining per-DOF advantage (+2.1%) comes from the minority of cases where uncertainty is truly dimension-selective: e.g., camera shift primarily affecting x/y while z remains well-estimated, or robot-state perturbation affecting specific joints. In these cases, per-DOF features allow CRM to correct the problematic dimensions without disturbing the accurate ones. The 2.1% gap (within [1,3]) is modest but positive, supporting the softened claim that \\\"per-DOF provides incremental gains over scalar.\\\" Most of the information-theoretic content of the 45D features is captured by the scalar summary — the per-DOF structure adds refinement rather than fundamentally new capability.\\n\"\n86\t  },\n87\t  {\n88\t    \"name\": \"ACR-VLA Stacked on SRPO\",\n89\t    \"description\": \"Train new CRM on SRPO-finetuned VLA checkpoint. New CGS calibration on SRPO samples (K=10). SRPO already partially addresses robustness via training-time optimization — question is whether inference-time conformal correction provides orthogonal value.\\n\",\n90\t    \"results\": {\n91\t      \"srpo_base_libero_plus\": 82.1,\n92\t      \"srpo_plus_acr_vla_libero_plus\": 85.0,\n93\t      \"srpo_stacking_gain_abs\": 2.9,\n94\t      \"srpo_plus_acr_vla_libero_nominal\": 93.1,\n95\t      \"srpo_base_libero_nominal\": 95.6,\n96\t      \"srpo_plus_acr_vla_camera\": 87.2,\n97\t      \"srpo_plus_acr_vla_noise\": 84.6,\n98\t      \"srpo_plus_acr_vla_robot_state\": 83.8,\n99\t      \"srpo_plus_acr_vla_lighting\": 85.4,\n100\t      \"srpo_plus_acr_vla_background\": 84.8,\n101\t      \"srpo_plus_acr_vla_layout\": 83.5,\n102\t      \"srpo_plus_acr_vla_language\": 83.2,\n103\t      \"crm_on_srpo_feature_quality_degradation_pct\": 22\n104\t    },\n105\t    \"tables\": [\n106\t      {\n107\t        \"name\": \"Table 2: SRPO Stacking Results\",\n108\t        \"content\": \"| Method              | LIBERO (nom.) | LIBERO-Plus | Δ Plus  |\\n|--------------------|---------------|-------------|---------|\\n| SmolVLA (base)     | 94.8          | 62.3        | —       |\\n| ACR-VLA            | 92.6          | 70.5        | +8.2    |\\n| SRPO               | 95.6          | 82.1        | +19.8   |\\n| SRPO + ACR-VLA     | 93.1          | 85.0        | +22.7   |\\n| Stacking gain      | -2.5          | +2.9        |         |\\n\"\n109\t      }\n110\t    ],\n111\t    \"figures\": [],\n112\t    \"analysis\": \"The 2.9% stacking gain survives the kill criterion (>2%) but barely. The paper should frame this cautiously: \\\"ACR-VLA provides orthogonal value even atop training-time optimization, though the margin is modest — consistent with SRPO having already resolved many of the uncertainty patterns that ACR-VLA targets.\\\" The 85.0% SOTA claim is legitimate but should acknowledge the narrow margin. Nominal drop of 2.5% on SRPO is slightly higher than on base VLA, possibly because SRPO's actions are more precise and CRM's residuals are more likely to degrade already-good actions.\\n\\n\\nSRPO's training-time optimization partially collapses the flow-matching sample diversity — the model is more deterministic post-finetuning, reducing the information content of K=10 samples. CGS features extracted from SRPO samples have ~22% lower variance compared to base VLA samples, meaning the uncertainty signal is attenuated. CRM still finds residual patterns to correct but the margin is narrower because SRPO has already addressed many of the systematic biases that CRM would otherwise correct. The 2.9% stacking gain (within [2,4]) comes from: (1) dimensions where SRPO's training augmentation didn't cover the specific perturbation pattern (e.g., specific camera angle combinations), and (2) high-uncertainty steps where SRPO's flow-matching still produces diffuse samples and CRM's conformal features provide useful correction signal. The gain is near but above the 2% kill threshold, supporting the claim \\\"complementary in principle, with modest empirical stacking gains.\\\" The 85.0% stacked result (within [84,86]) would be a new LIBERO-Plus SOTA.\\n\"\n113\t  },\n114\t  {\n115\t    \"name\": \"COC Signal Validation\",\n116\t    \"description\": \"Three-part validation of Chunk Overlap Consistency as self-referential ACI update signal: (a) Spearman ρ between episode-level COC coverage fraction and binary task success, (b) ACI alpha_t convergence trajectory over evaluation episodes, (c) Ellipsoid volume comparison between ACI-adaptive and static calibration at matched marginal coverage.\\n\",\n117\t    \"results\": {\n118\t      \"coc_success_spearman_rho\": 0.38,\n119\t      \"coc_success_spearman_p_value\": 0.003,\n120\t      \"aci_convergence_episodes_to_0p02\": 148,\n121\t      \"aci_target_coverage\": 0.9,\n122\t      \"aci_achieved_coverage_at_convergence\": 0.89,\n123\t      \"aci_overshoot_peak_alpha\": 0.22,\n124\t      \"aci_steady_state_alpha\": 0.11,\n125\t      \"ellipsoid_volume_ratio_adaptive_vs_static\": 0.847,\n126\t      \"ellipsoid_volume_narrowing_pct\": 15.3,\n127\t      \"static_calibration_coverage_achieved\": 0.9,\n128\t      \"adaptive_calibration_coverage_achieved\": 0.89\n129\t    },\n130\t    \"tables\": [\n131\t      {\n132\t        \"name\": \"Table 3: COC Signal Quality\",\n133\t        \"content\": \"| Metric                              | Value  |\\n|-------------------------------------|--------|\\n| Spearman ρ (COC ↔ success)          | 0.38   |\\n| p-value                              | 0.003  |\\n| ACI convergence (episodes to ±0.02) | 148    |\\n| ACI achieved coverage               | 0.89   |\\n| Ellipsoid narrowing vs static        | 15.3%  |\\n\"\n134\t      }\n135\t    ],\n136\t    \"figures\": [],\n137\t    \"analysis\": \"COC is validated as a positive but weak signal. ρ=0.38 is statistically significant (p=0.003) but far from the \\\"reliable\\\" threshold that would make COC a standalone monitoring tool. The paper should position COC as: \\\"a step toward fully self-referential uncertainty monitoring that shows promise but requires further development for deployment-grade reliability.\\\" ACI convergence demonstrates the theoretical guarantee holds in practice, but the slow convergence and overshoot suggest that COC's noisy signal makes online adaptation sluggish. The ellipsoid narrowing result is the strongest evidence for ACI's value — same coverage with tighter sets means more informative uncertainty features for CRM.\\n\\n\\nCOC provides a per-step proxy for whether the model's predictions are self-consistent across overlapping action chunks — high overlap agreement correlates with accurate predictions, low agreement with uncertain/inaccurate ones. However, the correlation with task success is attenuated (ρ=0.38, within [0.3, 0.5]) because: (1) task success is binary while COC is continuous, creating threshold effects; (2) a single bad step can fail a task regardless of high average COC; (3) some tasks succeed despite low COC through robustness of the underlying dynamics. The positive but weak correlation means COC is a \\\"promising\\\" self-referential signal but not \\\"reliable\\\" enough to be the primary ACI driver — episode-level success (when available) would be more informative. ACI converges but slowly (~148 episodes, within expected range for weak signal) with initial overshoot (alpha peaks at 0.22 before settling to 0.11) because COC-based updates have high variance. The 15.3% ellipsoid narrowing (within [10, 20]) demonstrates that adaptive calibration produces tighter prediction sets than static — same coverage guarantee but more informative uncertainty regions. The narrowing is modest because K=10 covariance estimates limit how sharp the ellipsoids can get.\\n\"\n138\t  },\n139\t  {\n140\t    \"name\": \"A1 Ablation — CRM without ACI (Static Calibration)\",\n141\t    \"description\": \"Full CRM with per-DOF conformal features, but CGS thresholds fixed at calibration-set values — no online ACI adaptation. COC is not computed. Tests the marginal contribution of ACI.\\n\",\n142\t    \"results\": {\n143\t      \"libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate\": 67.8,\n144\t      \"libero_plus_gain_over_base\": 5.5,\n145\t      \"a1_gap_to_full_acr_vla\": 2.7,\n146\t      \"libero_nominal_success_rate\": 93.4,\n147\t      \"static_coverage_at_early_episodes\": 0.93,\n148\t      \"static_coverage_at_late_episodes\": 0.82\n149\t    },\n150\t    \"tables\": [\n151\t      {\n152\t        \"name\": \"Ablation Table Row (A1)\",\n153\t        \"content\": \"| Variant                | LIBERO-Plus | Δ vs Full |\\n|-----------------------|-------------|-----------|\\n| A1: CRM, static CGS  | 67.8        | -2.7      |\\n| Full ACR-VLA          | 70.5        | —         |\\n\"\n154\t      }\n155\t    ],\n156\t    \"figures\": [],\n157\t    \"analysis\": \"ACI contributes ~2.7% absolute, which is meaningful but secondary to the conformal features themselves. The coverage drift analysis (0.93→0.82 for static) provides a clean mechanistic explanation for why adaptive calibration helps. This supports Claim 2 but with caveats — ACI is beneficial but not transformative.\\n\\n\\nWithout ACI, conformal thresholds are calibrated on the training distribution and remain fixed. Under LIBERO-Plus perturbations, the calibration set is no longer representative: early evaluation episodes see conservative over-coverage (0.93 vs target 0.90) because perturbations haven't fully diverged from calibration conditions, but later episodes see under-coverage (0.82) as distribution shift accumulates. This miscalibration means CRM receives systematically biased uncertainty features — over-estimated for some perturbation types, under-estimated for others. The 2.7% gap to full ACR-VLA reflects that ACI's online correction produces better-calibrated inputs for CRM, particularly for perturbation dimensions where the shift is gradual and ACI can track it (camera, lighting). For abrupt shifts (layout, language), ACI has less time to adapt, so the gap is smaller there. The result validates ACI's contribution but shows it's not the dominant factor — static conformal features still carry most of the correction signal.\\n\"\n158\t  },\n159\t  {\n160\t    \"name\": \"A2 Ablation — ACI without CRM (Sample Selection Only)\",\n161\t    \"description\": \"CGS + COC + ACI pipeline but instead of CRM, uses multivariate-CP-guided sample selection (extend ReconVLA's approach to leverage per-DOF conformal radii for selecting best sample from K=10). No learned correction, just informed selection.\\n\",\n162\t    \"results\": {\n163\t      \"libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate\": 66.9,\n164\t      \"libero_plus_gain_over_base\": 4.6,\n165\t      \"a2_gap_to_full_acr_vla\": 3.6,\n166\t      \"libero_nominal_success_rate\": 94.2\n167\t    },\n168\t    \"tables\": [\n169\t      {\n170\t        \"name\": \"Ablation Table Row (A2)\",\n171\t        \"content\": \"| Variant               | LIBERO-Plus | LIBERO (nom.) | Δ Plus vs Full |\\n|----------------------|-------------|---------------|----------------|\\n| A2: Selection only   | 66.9        | 94.2          | -3.6           |\\n| Full ACR-VLA         | 70.5        | 92.6          | —              |\\n\"\n172\t      }\n173\t    ],\n174\t    \"figures\": [],\n175\t    \"analysis\": \"A2 provides a useful reference point — it validates that CGS features enable better sample selection (extending ReconVLA), and quantifies CRM's value as a learned corrector (+3.6%). The nominal preservation advantage of A2 is worth discussing as a practical deployment consideration: if nominal drop must be minimized, selection-only is safer.\\n\\n\\nSample selection with multivariate CP features is a strict improvement over ReconVLA's scalar selection — per-DOF conformal radii enable selecting the sample that is best specifically in the most-uncertain dimensions, rather than best in aggregate Euclidean distance. The 4.6% gain over base is substantial and validates that CGS features contain useful information. However, selection is fundamentally limited to choosing among the K=10 samples — it cannot synthesize an action better than any individual sample, which is CRM's advantage. The 3.6% gap to full ACR-VLA quantifies CRM's contribution as a learned synthesizer. Notably, the 0.6% nominal drop is much smaller than full ACR-VLA's 2.2% — because sample selection is conservative (it only picks from generated samples, never modifying them), it's less likely to degrade in-distribution performance. This creates an interesting trade-off for the paper to discuss.\\n\"\n176\t  },\n177\t  {\n178\t    \"name\": \"Per-Perturbation-Dimension Breakdown\",\n179\t    \"description\": \"Detailed ACR-VLA gain analysis across 7 LIBERO-Plus perturbation dimensions, evaluated per-task (10 tasks × 7 dimensions = 70 cells). Statistical significance via paired bootstrap (1000 resamples, α=0.05) over 5 seeds.\\n\",\n180\t    \"results\": {\n181\t      \"camera_shift_gain\": 12.6,\n182\t      \"camera_shift_p_value\": 0.001,\n183\t      \"sensor_noise_gain\": 10.8,\n184\t      \"sensor_noise_p_value\": 0.002,\n185\t      \"robot_state_gain\": 8.5,\n186\t      \"robot_state_p_value\": 0.008,\n187\t      \"lighting_gain\": 5.8,\n188\t      \"lighting_p_value\": 0.031,\n189\t      \"background_gain\": 3.2,\n190\t      \"background_p_value\": 0.112,\n191\t      \"layout_gain\": 0.9,\n192\t      \"layout_p_value\": 0.483,\n193\t      \"language_gain\": 0.9,\n194\t      \"language_p_value\": 0.501,\n195\t      \"dims_with_significant_gain\": 4,\n196\t      \"dims_significant_list\": \"camera_shift, sensor_noise, robot_state, lighting\"\n197\t    },\n198\t    \"tables\": [\n199\t      {\n200\t        \"name\": \"Table 4: Per-Perturbation ACR-VLA Gains\",\n201\t        \"content\": \"| Perturbation   | Base  | ACR-VLA | Δ     | p-value | Sig.? |\\n|---------------|-------|---------|-------|---------|-------|\\n| camera_shift  | 58.2  | 70.8    | +12.6 | 0.001   | ✓     |\\n| sensor_noise  | 55.6  | 66.4    | +10.8 | 0.002   | ✓     |\\n| robot_state   | 56.8  | 65.3    | +8.5  | 0.008   | ✓     |\\n| lighting      | 65.4  | 71.2    | +5.8  | 0.031   | ✓     |\\n| background    | 68.3  | 71.5    | +3.2  | 0.112   | ✗     |\\n| layout        | 72.1  | 73.0    | +0.9  | 0.483   | ✗     |\\n| language      | 71.5  | 72.4    | +0.9  | 0.501   | ✗     |\\n\"\n202\t      },\n203\t      {\n204\t        \"name\": \"Table 4b: Per-Task × Per-Perturbation Heatmap Data (top-5 tasks by gain)\",\n205\t        \"content\": \"| Task              | camera | noise | robot | light | bg   | layout | lang |\\n|-------------------|--------|-------|-------|-------|------|--------|------|\\n| pick_place_can    | +18.0  | +14.0 | +12.0 | +8.0  | +4.0 | +2.0   | +0.0 |\\n| open_drawer       | +16.0  | +12.0 | +10.0 | +6.0  | +4.0 | +0.0   | +2.0 |\\n| stack_blocks      | +14.0  | +14.0 | +10.0 | +8.0  | +2.0 | +0.0   | +0.0 |\\n| close_microwave   | +12.0  | +10.0 | +8.0  | +6.0  | +4.0 | +2.0   | +0.0 |\\n| turn_faucet       | +10.0  | +8.0  | +6.0  | +4.0  | +2.0 | +0.0   | +2.0 |\\n\"\n206\t      }\n207\t    ],\n208\t    \"figures\": [],\n209\t    \"analysis\": \"4/7 dimensions with significant gain is within [3, 5]. This result is central to the median-tier narrative: ACR-VLA works well where calibration coverage exists, honestly fails where it doesn't. The paper should present this transparency as a strength — \\\"we clearly identify the boundary of our method's applicability and provide a mechanistic explanation.\\\" The correlation between augmentation coverage and gain (r=0.91) is a strong explanatory result that reviewers will appreciate. Layout/language as honest limitations, with ACI online adaptation as partial mitigation and future work on semantic augmentation as natural extension.\\n\\n\\nThe per-perturbation structure directly reflects synthetic augmentation coverage. Camera jitter, Gaussian sensor noise, and robot-state perturbation are all covered by synthetic augmentation — CGS sees similar distribution shifts during calibration, producing well-calibrated conformal features that CRM can exploit. Camera shift shows the largest gain (+12.6%) because it primarily affects translational DOFs (x, y) where per-DOF conformal features are most discriminative — camera shift makes position uncertain while orientation remains relatively stable, and per-DOF features capture this asymmetry. Lighting (+5.8%) reaches significance because brightness changes create predictable patterns in flow-matching sample variance. Background changes (+3.2%) cause modest, diffuse uncertainty increase that CRM partially addresses, but the effect is not statistically significant at α=0.05. Layout (+0.9%) and language (+0.9%) are essentially unaddressed because these are semantic shifts that synthetic augmentation cannot cover — the calibration set contains no examples of rearranged furniture or paraphrased instructions, so CGS features under these perturbations are poorly calibrated and CRM cannot extract useful correction signal.\\n\"\n210\t  },\n211\t  {\n212\t    \"name\": \"A5 Ablation — CGS Joint Mahalanobis vs Per-DOF Bonferroni\",\n213\t    \"description\": \"Compare full CGS (joint Mahalanobis with Ledoit-Wolf shrunk covariance) vs per-DOF independent Bonferroni-corrected intervals. Tests whether multivariate CP's joint structure provides value beyond simply having per-DOF information.\\n\",\n214\t    \"results\": {\n215\t      \"joint_mahalanobis_libero_plus\": 70.5,\n216\t      \"bonferroni_libero_plus\": 69.1,\n217\t      \"gap_joint_vs_bonferroni\": 1.4,\n218\t      \"bonferroni_coverage\": 0.94,\n219\t      \"joint_coverage\": 0.9,\n220\t      \"bonferroni_avg_ellipsoid_volume\": 1.0,\n221\t      \"joint_avg_ellipsoid_volume\": 0.72\n222\t    },\n223\t    \"tables\": [\n224\t      {\n225\t        \"name\": \"A5 Ablation Results\",\n226\t        \"content\": \"| CGS Variant         | LIBERO-Plus | Coverage | Avg. Volume |\\n|--------------------|-------------|----------|-------------|\\n| Joint Mahalanobis  | 70.5        | 0.90     | 0.72 (norm) |\\n| Per-DOF Bonferroni | 69.1        | 0.94     | 1.00 (norm) |\\n\"\n227\t      }\n228\t    ],\n229\t    \"figures\": [],\n230\t    \"analysis\": \"The 1.4% gap is small but validates that joint multivariate CP provides value through covariance-aware regions. Worth reporting as a supplementary ablation to support the multivariate CP design choice. The over-coverage of Bonferroni (0.94 vs target 0.90) is an inherent inefficiency that the joint approach avoids.\\n\\n\\nBonferroni correction treats each DOF independently and inflates thresholds to maintain joint coverage — producing conservative, hypercubic confidence regions that ignore cross-DOF covariance. Joint Mahalanobis with shrunk covariance produces ellipsoidal regions aligned with the actual covariance structure, yielding tighter (28% smaller volume) regions at the same coverage level. The 1.4% performance gap comes from CRM receiving more informative features: joint CGS features encode which DOF pairs co-vary (e.g., x and y under camera shift), enabling CRM to make correlated corrections across related DOFs. With Bonferroni, CRM sees each DOF's uncertainty independently and must re-learn any cross-DOF correction patterns from the observation embedding alone. The gap is modest because K=10 Ledoit-Wolf covariance is noisy — the theoretical advantage of joint Mahalanobis is attenuated by covariance estimation error, and Bonferroni's conservative margins partially compensate by providing wider \\\"safety padding.\\\"\\n\"\n231\t  },\n232\t  {\n233\t    \"name\": \"A6 Ablation — COC Signal vs Episode-Level Success for ACI\",\n234\t    \"description\": \"Compare ACI driven by COC (self-referential, per-step) vs ACI driven by episode-level binary task success (stronger signal, but requires ground-truth outcome and only updates once per episode).\\n\",\n235\t    \"results\": {\n236\t      \"coc_based_aci_libero_plus\": 70.5,\n237\t      \"episode_based_aci_libero_plus\": 71.8,\n238\t      \"gap_episode_vs_coc\": 1.3,\n239\t      \"coc_aci_convergence_episodes\": 148,\n240\t      \"episode_aci_convergence_episodes\": 42,\n241\t      \"coc_aci_coverage_variance\": 0.018,\n242\t      \"episode_aci_coverage_variance\": 0.006\n243\t    },\n244\t    \"tables\": [\n245\t      {\n246\t        \"name\": \"A6 Ablation: ACI Signal Comparison\",\n247\t        \"content\": \"| ACI Signal     | LIBERO-Plus | Convergence (eps) | Coverage Var. |\\n|---------------|-------------|-------------------|---------------|\\n| COC (self-ref)| 70.5        | 148               | 0.018         |\\n| Episode success| 71.8       | 42                | 0.006         |\\n\"\n248\t      }\n249\t    ],\n250\t    \"figures\": [],\n251\t    \"analysis\": \"Episode-based ACI is clearly superior when ground-truth is available. COC's value proposition is self-referentiality for deployment scenarios without outcome feedback. The 1.3% gap and 3.5x convergence slowdown are honest costs of self-referentiality. Paper recommendation: use episode-level ACI as the default in simulation experiments; present COC as a deployment-oriented variant with known performance trade-offs. This supports repositioning COC as \\\"promising future work\\\" rather than the primary ACI signal.\\n\\n\\nEpisode-level success is a stronger, lower-variance signal for ACI — it directly reflects whether the uncertainty calibration led to good outcomes. COC is noisy because chunk overlap consistency is only a proxy for prediction quality, and individual step-level COC values have high variance. The 1.3% gap reflects that episode-based ACI converges ~3.5x faster (42 vs 148 episodes) and maintains tighter coverage tracking (variance 0.006 vs 0.018). However, episode-level success requires ground-truth task outcomes — available in simulation but not in real deployment. This is the key argument for COC: it's self-referential (no external supervision). The paper should present this as a trade-off: \\\"episode-level ACI is preferable when outcomes are available; COC-based ACI sacrifices ~1.3% performance for full self-referentiality.\\\" In the median tier, COC's disadvantage is noticeable but not disqualifying — it still converges and provides meaningful adaptation, just more slowly and with more variance.\\n\"\n252\t  },\n253\t  {\n254\t    \"name\": \"A7 Ablation — DiU Aleatoric vs CGS Epistemic Features\",\n255\t    \"description\": \"Replace CGS epistemic uncertainty features (45D) with DiU-style aleatoric uncertainty features (7D per-DOF variance from single forward pass). Also test combined epistemic+aleatoric (52D). Validates that epistemic (distribution-shift-sensitive) features are more useful than aleatoric (in-distribution-also-high) features for OOD correction.\\n\",\n256\t    \"results\": {\n257\t      \"cgs_epistemic_libero_plus\": 70.5,\n258\t      \"diu_aleatoric_libero_plus\": 67.4,\n259\t      \"combined_52d_libero_plus\": 71.1,\n260\t      \"gap_epistemic_vs_aleatoric\": 3.1,\n261\t      \"cgs_epistemic_libero_nominal\": 92.6,\n262\t      \"diu_aleatoric_libero_nominal\": 93.9,\n263\t      \"combined_52d_libero_nominal\": 92.4,\n264\t      \"diu_aleatoric_in_dist_variance\": 0.048,\n265\t      \"cgs_epistemic_in_dist_variance\": 0.012,\n266\t      \"diu_aleatoric_ood_variance\": 0.063,\n267\t      \"cgs_epistemic_ood_variance\": 0.089\n268\t    },\n269\t    \"tables\": [\n270\t      {\n271\t        \"name\": \"A7 Ablation: Feature Type Comparison\",\n272\t        \"content\": \"| Feature Type        | Dim | LIBERO (nom.) | LIBERO-Plus | Δ Plus vs Base |\\n|--------------------|-----|---------------|-------------|----------------|\\n| CGS epistemic       | 45  | 92.6          | 70.5        | +8.2           |\\n| DiU aleatoric       | 7   | 93.9          | 67.4        | +5.1           |\\n| Combined            | 52  | 92.4          | 71.1        | +8.8           |\\n\"\n273\t      },\n274\t      {\n275\t        \"name\": \"A7: Signal Discrimination\",\n276\t        \"content\": \"| Feature Source | In-Dist Var. | OOD Var. | Ratio (OOD/ID) |\\n|--------------|-------------|---------|----------------|\\n| CGS epistemic | 0.012       | 0.089   | 7.42           |\\n| DiU aleatoric | 0.048       | 0.063   | 1.31           |\\n\"\n277\t      }\n278\t    ],\n279\t    \"figures\": [],\n280\t    \"analysis\": \"The 7.42x OOD/ID ratio for epistemic features vs 1.31x for aleatoric validates the core design choice. This is one of the cleaner results — it mechanistically explains why flow-matching sample diversity (epistemic) is more informative than single-pass variance (aleatoric) for distribution shift detection. The combined 52D result (+0.6% over epistemic alone) suggests that adding aleatoric features is marginally beneficial — worth mentioning but not worth the complexity for most applications.\\n\\n\\nDiU captures aleatoric uncertainty — inherent action ambiguity that exists even in-distribution. Under distribution shift, DiU's signal increases only modestly (+31%, 0.048→0.063) because most of what DiU measures is irreducible noise. CGS epistemic features, in contrast, are low in-distribution (0.012) and spike dramatically under shift (+642%, 0.012→0.089), providing a clear signal for \\\"this is out-of-distribution.\\\" CRM conditioned on epistemic features can discriminate OOD steps that need correction from in-distribution steps that don't. With aleatoric features, CRM sees consistently elevated uncertainty everywhere, reducing its ability to be selective. The 3.1% gap validates the epistemic-vs-aleatoric distinction. The combined 52D features provide a marginal +0.6% over epistemic alone — aleatoric features add some complementary information about inherent action ambiguity (useful for multi-modal tasks like placing objects with rotational symmetry), but the gain is small because most of the relevant signal is already captured by epistemic features. The lower nominal drop of DiU (0.9% vs 2.2% for CGS) reflects that aleatoric features are less \\\"aggressive\\\" — they don't provoke large corrections because their signal doesn't differentiate ID from OOD.\\n\"\n281\t  },\n282\t  {\n283\t    \"name\": \"Feature Importance Analysis\",\n284\t    \"description\": \"Gradient-based feature importance: compute |∂correction/∂feature| averaged over evaluation episodes, broken down by perturbation type. Identifies which of the 45 CGS features drive CRM's corrections for each perturbation dimension.\\n\",\n285\t    \"results\": {\n286\t      \"top_features_camera_shift\": \"per-DOF std (x, y) — 34% of total gradient; conformal radius (x, y) — 22%\",\n287\t      \"top_features_sensor_noise\": \"per-DOF std (all DOFs) — 41%; ellipsoid volume — 18%\",\n288\t      \"top_features_robot_state\": \"conformal radius (joint 1-4) — 38%; per-DOF std (joint 1-4) — 25%\",\n289\t      \"top_features_lighting\": \"per-DOF std (x, y, z) — 29%; ellipsoid volume — 21%\",\n290\t      \"per_dof_std_importance_share\": 0.42,\n291\t      \"conformal_radius_importance_share\": 0.31,\n292\t      \"ellipsoid_volume_importance_share\": 0.14,\n293\t      \"cross_dof_covariance_importance_share\": 0.13\n294\t    },\n295\t    \"tables\": [\n296\t      {\n297\t        \"name\": \"Table 5: Feature Importance by Category\",\n298\t        \"content\": \"| Feature Category    | Importance Share | Primary Perturbations |\\n|--------------------|-----------------|----------------------|\\n| Per-DOF std (7D)   | 42%             | noise, camera, light |\\n| Conformal radius (7D)| 31%           | robot-state, camera  |\\n| Ellipsoid volume (1D)| 14%           | all (gating)         |\\n| Cross-DOF cov (30D) | 13%            | robot-state          |\\n\"\n299\t      }\n300\t    ],\n301\t    \"figures\": [],\n302\t    \"analysis\": \"The feature importance analysis validates the dimension-selective correction mechanism: CRM learns to attend to different uncertainty features for different perturbation types, without explicit supervision about which perturbation is active. This is a compelling interpretability result for the paper. The 42%+31% = 73% importance of per-DOF features (std + conformal radius) supports Claim 1's emphasis on per-DOF structure, even though the A4 ablation showed only +2.1% over scalar — the mechanism is clearly there, even if the aggregate performance gain is modest.\\n\\n\\nPer-DOF std features are the most important (42% of gradient magnitude) because they directly encode \\\"how uncertain is this specific DOF\\\" — the most actionable information for dimension-selective correction. Conformal radius features rank second (31%) because they encode calibrated confidence intervals that are more robust than raw std to distributional assumptions. Ellipsoid volume (14%) is a global uncertainty summary — useful for CRM's gating mechanism (how much total correction to apply) but less informative for dimension selection. Cross-DOF covariance features (13%) capture correlation structure; their moderate importance reflects that K=10 covariance estimates are noisy, limiting the information content of off-diagonal terms. The perturbation-specific patterns are illuminating: camera shift activates translational DOF features (x, y) because it primarily affects workspace position estimation; robot-state perturbation activates joint-specific conformal radii because it directly perturbs joint configurations; sensor noise activates broad std features because it diffusely increases all DOF uncertainties.\\n\"\n303\t  },\n304\t  {\n305\t    \"name\": \"SnapFlow Integration (K=10 × 1-step)\",\n306\t    \"description\": \"Replace K=10 full denoising trajectories with K=10 single-step distilled generation via SnapFlow-style consistency distillation. Test: (a) inference speedup, (b) sample diversity preservation, (c) downstream ACR-VLA performance.\\n\",\n307\t    \"results\": {\n308\t      \"full_denoising_inference_ms\": 118,\n309\t      \"snapflow_1step_inference_ms\": 38,\n310\t      \"speedup_factor\": 3.1,\n311\t      \"diversity_trace_ratio\": 0.83,\n312\t      \"full_denoising_libero_plus\": 70.5,\n313\t      \"snapflow_libero_plus\": 69.8,\n314\t      \"performance_drop\": 0.7,\n315\t      \"snapflow_covariance_frobenius_error\": 0.21\n316\t    },\n317\t    \"tables\": [\n318\t      {\n319\t        \"name\": \"Table S1: SnapFlow Integration\",\n320\t        \"content\": \"| Sampling Method     | K=10 Time (ms) | Diversity | LIBERO-Plus | Δ   |\\n|--------------------|----------------|-----------|-------------|-----|\\n| Full denoising     | 118            | 1.00      | 70.5        | —   |\\n| SnapFlow 1-step    | 38             | 0.83      | 69.8        | -0.7|\\n\"\n321\t      }\n322\t    ],\n323\t    \"figures\": [],\n324\t    \"analysis\": \"SnapFlow integration is a practical engineering contribution. The 0.7% drop is small enough that the 3.1x speedup is clearly worthwhile for deployment. The diversity reduction is the primary concern — if future work increases K beyond 10, SnapFlow's diversity loss may compound. Report as supplementary result demonstrating deployment feasibility.\\n\\nSingle-step distilled generation produces samples ~3.1x faster but with reduced diversity: the trace ratio of 0.83 means 1-step samples capture 83% of the covariance structure of full denoising samples. The 17% diversity loss comes from the distillation process collapsing minor modes in the action distribution — the dominant mode is well-preserved but tail samples are pulled inward. This propagates to a modest 0.7% ACR-VLA performance drop because the CGS features computed from less-diverse samples have slightly attenuated covariance structure, reducing the per-DOF signal quality. The Frobenius error of 0.21 (normalized) in covariance estimation means the Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage target absorbs more weight, further compressing the estimated covariance toward the identity-like shrinkage target. The practical trade-off is favorable: 3.1x speedup for 0.7% accuracy cost, making real-time deployment more feasible. This is a supplementary result that strengthens the practical narrative without affecting claims.\\n\"\n325\t  }\n326\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"674be60e-e4c3-45c8-be6d-42aa229c3df7","uuid":"bec3d5ae-4d08-4715-9e2b-bc77880d783b","timestamp":"2026-05-13T02:57:56.178Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_8/projects/acr-vla-conformal-refinement/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T023849/median/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"ACR-VLA Full Pipeline (SmolVLA base)\",\n    \"description\": \"Full ACR-VLA pipeline: CGS (K=10, Ledoit-Wolf) + ACI/COC + CRM (131K params). CRM trained on LIBERO demos + synthetic augmentation (camera jitter, Gaussian noise, robot-state perturbation). Evaluated on LIBERO nominal (10 tasks) and LIBERO-Plus (7 perturbation dimensions × 10 tasks).\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"libero_nominal_success_rate\": 92.6,\n      \"libero_nominal_base\": 94.8,\n      \"libero_nominal_drop\": 2.2,\n      \"libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate\": 70.5,\n      \"libero_plus_base\": 62.3,\n      \"libero_plus_gain_abs\": 8.2,\n      \"libero_plus_camera_shift\": 70.8,\n      \"libero_plus_sensor_noise\": 66.4,\n      \"libero_plus_robot_state\": 65.3,\n      \"libero_plus_lighting\": 71.2,\n      \"libero_plus_background\": 71.5,\n      \"libero_plus_layout\": 73.0,\n      \"libero_plus_language\": 72.4,\n      \"crm_inference_overhead_ms\": 3.2,\n      \"cgs_k10_sampling_time_ms\": 118,\n      \"total_inference_time_per_step_ms\": 134\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 1: LIBERO and LIBERO-Plus Success Rates\",\n        \"content\": \"| Method       | LIBERO (nominal) | LIBERO-Plus (agg.) | Δ Plus |\\n|-------------|------------------|-------------------|--------|\\n| SmolVLA     | 94.8             | 62.3              | —      |\\n| ACR-VLA     | 92.6             | 70.5              | +8.2   |\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 1 (extended): Per-Perturbation Breakdown\",\n        \"content\": \"| Perturbation  | SmolVLA | ACR-VLA | Δ     | Significant? |\\n|--------------|---------|---------|-------|-------------|\\n| camera_shift | 58.2    | 70.8    | +12.6 | ✓           |\\n| sensor_noise | 55.6    | 66.4    | +10.8 | ✓           |\\n| robot_state  | 56.8    | 65.3    | +8.5  | ✓           |\\n| lighting     | 65.4    | 71.2    | +5.8  | ✓           |\\n| background   | 68.3    | 71.5    | +3.2  | ✗           |\\n| layout       | 72.1    | 73.0    | +0.9  | ✗           |\\n| language     | 71.5    | 72.4    | +0.9  | ✗           |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The 8.2% aggregate gain is solidly within the median range [6, 11]. The per-perturbation structure is informative: the 4 dimensions with significant gain (camera, noise, robot-state, lighting) are precisely those covered by synthetic augmentation, confirming that CGS calibration quality is the bottleneck. Layout and language perturbations involve semantic shifts that synthetic augmentation cannot cover — CGS features for these are essentially noise, and CRM correctly learns to make near-zero corrections. The 2.2% nominal drop is acceptable for CoRL but should be discussed honestly.\\n\\n\\nFlow-matching samples from real VLA have covariance structure but noisier than synthetic MVP: K=10 Ledoit-Wolf estimates are adequate but not tight, introducing ~15-20% feature quality degradation vs oracle covariance. CRM still learns meaningful correction patterns because per-DOF std and conformal radii carry genuine distributional information even with shrunk covariance. The 8.2% aggregate gain comes primarily from camera/noise/robot-state dimensions where synthetic augmentation provides good calibration-set coverage — CRM learns to suppress high-uncertainty DOFs (typically wrist rotation and z-translation under camera shift) while preserving confident DOFs. The 2.2% nominal drop is CRM occasionally over-correcting in-distribution actions where CGS correctly reports low uncertainty but CRM's learned residual still nudges slightly; this is the cost of the correction module's expressivity. Gain is real but not as dramatic as the MVP's ~27% per-DOF advantage because real VLA action distributions are higher-dimensional (7-DOF chunks vs 3D), multimodal, and the covariance structure is harder to estimate reliably with K=10.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"A3 Ablation — CRM without Conformal Features\",\n    \"description\": \"CRM receives only observation embeddings (from VLA encoder) as input, no CGS-derived uncertainty features. Equivalent to an A2C2-style generic learned residual policy. Same CRM architecture (131K params), same training data and procedure as full ACR-VLA.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate\": 65.8,\n      \"libero_plus_gain_over_base\": 3.5,\n      \"a3_gap_to_full_acr_vla\": 4.7,\n      \"libero_nominal_success_rate\": 93.8,\n      \"libero_plus_camera_shift\": 63.1,\n      \"libero_plus_sensor_noise\": 60.2,\n      \"libero_plus_robot_state\": 60.9,\n      \"libero_plus_lighting\": 67.8,\n      \"libero_plus_background\": 69.5,\n      \"libero_plus_layout\": 72.6,\n      \"libero_plus_language\": 71.8\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Ablation Table Row (A3)\",\n        \"content\": \"| Variant              | LIBERO-Plus | Δ vs Base | Δ vs Full ACR-VLA |\\n|---------------------|-------------|-----------|-------------------|\\n| SmolVLA (base)      | 62.3        | —         | -8.2              |\\n| A3: CRM, no unc.   | 65.8        | +3.5      | -4.7              |\\n| Full ACR-VLA        | 70.5        | +8.2      | —                 |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"A3 gap of 4.7% clearly exceeds the 2% kill threshold. Claim 1 survives but the narrative must acknowledge that observation-conditioned residuals alone provide non-trivial value (+3.5%). The story should frame this as: \\\"CRM provides a correction mechanism; conformal features provide the information that makes the correction dimension-selective and magnitude-appropriate.\\\"\\n\\n\\nWithout uncertainty features, CRM can only learn a fixed residual correction pattern conditioned on visual observations. It captures some systematic biases (e.g., consistent z-offset under camera shift) but cannot adapt correction magnitude or direction to per-step uncertainty. The 3.5% gain over base reflects that observation-conditioned residuals capture low-hanging fruit — average correction vectors that are right more often than wrong. But the lack of dimension-selective information means CRM applies similar corrections to confident and uncertain DOFs alike, sometimes degrading already-accurate dimensions. The 4.7% gap to full ACR-VLA (within [3,7]) confirms that conformal features provide substantial value — almost as much as the residual module itself — but the gap isn't overwhelming, consistent with the median tier's \\\"per-DOF advantage exists but attenuated\\\" characterization. The kill criterion (gap < 2%) is clearly survived; Claim 1 holds with the softened framing.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"A4 Ablation — Scalar Uncertainty Features\",\n    \"description\": \"CRM receives observation embeddings + scalar uncertainty features (Euclidean distance from mean to nearest calibration point, analogous to ReconVLA's approach) instead of 45D per-DOF features. Scalar features: 1D distance + 1D log-likelihood + 1D normalized rank = 3D total.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate\": 68.4,\n      \"libero_plus_gain_over_base\": 6.1,\n      \"a4_gap_to_full_acr_vla\": 2.1,\n      \"a4_gap_to_a3\": 2.6,\n      \"libero_nominal_success_rate\": 93.2,\n      \"libero_plus_camera_shift\": 67.5,\n      \"libero_plus_sensor_noise\": 63.8,\n      \"libero_plus_robot_state\": 63.0,\n      \"libero_plus_lighting\": 69.8,\n      \"libero_plus_background\": 70.4,\n      \"libero_plus_layout\": 72.8,\n      \"libero_plus_language\": 72.1\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Ablation Table Row (A4)\",\n        \"content\": \"| Variant              | LIBERO-Plus | Δ vs Base | Δ vs A3 | Δ vs Full |\\n|---------------------|-------------|-----------|---------|-----------|\\n| SmolVLA (base)      | 62.3        | —         | —       | -8.2      |\\n| A3: CRM, no unc.   | 65.8        | +3.5      | —       | -4.7      |\\n| A4: CRM + scalar   | 68.4        | +6.1      | +2.6    | -2.1      |\\n| Full ACR-VLA        | 70.5        | +8.2      | +4.7    | —         |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The A4 gap of 2.1% is within [1,3] as expected for median tier. The critical insight for the paper: the decomposition base→A3→A4→Full shows gains of +3.5/+2.6/+2.1. The transition from \\\"no uncertainty\\\" to \\\"any uncertainty\\\" (+2.6) is larger than \\\"scalar to per-DOF\\\" (+2.1), but per-DOF still provides meaningful additional value. The narrative should emphasize that all three components contribute and per-DOF is the final piece that enables truly dimension-selective correction.\\n\\n\\nScalar uncertainty gives CRM a \\\"how uncertain overall\\\" signal, enabling it to modulate correction magnitude globally — correct more aggressively when scalar uncertainty is high, less when low. This captures the bulk of the uncertainty-conditioning benefit (+2.6% over A3), because the dominant failure mode under distribution shift is \\\"everything is somewhat off\\\" rather than \\\"one specific DOF is wrong.\\\" The remaining per-DOF advantage (+2.1%) comes from the minority of cases where uncertainty is truly dimension-selective: e.g., camera shift primarily affecting x/y while z remains well-estimated, or robot-state perturbation affecting specific joints. In these cases, per-DOF features allow CRM to correct the problematic dimensions without disturbing the accurate ones. The 2.1% gap (within [1,3]) is modest but positive, supporting the softened claim that \\\"per-DOF provides incremental gains over scalar.\\\" Most of the information-theoretic content of the 45D features is captured by the scalar summary — the per-DOF structure adds refinement rather than fundamentally new capability.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"ACR-VLA Stacked on SRPO\",\n    \"description\": \"Train new CRM on SRPO-finetuned VLA checkpoint. New CGS calibration on SRPO samples (K=10). SRPO already partially addresses robustness via training-time optimization — question is whether inference-time conformal correction provides orthogonal value.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"srpo_base_libero_plus\": 82.1,\n      \"srpo_plus_acr_vla_libero_plus\": 85.0,\n      \"srpo_stacking_gain_abs\": 2.9,\n      \"srpo_plus_acr_vla_libero_nominal\": 93.1,\n      \"srpo_base_libero_nominal\": 95.6,\n      \"srpo_plus_acr_vla_camera\": 87.2,\n      \"srpo_plus_acr_vla_noise\": 84.6,\n      \"srpo_plus_acr_vla_robot_state\": 83.8,\n      \"srpo_plus_acr_vla_lighting\": 85.4,\n      \"srpo_plus_acr_vla_background\": 84.8,\n      \"srpo_plus_acr_vla_layout\": 83.5,\n      \"srpo_plus_acr_vla_language\": 83.2,\n      \"crm_on_srpo_feature_quality_degradation_pct\": 22\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 2: SRPO Stacking Results\",\n        \"content\": \"| Method              | LIBERO (nom.) | LIBERO-Plus | Δ Plus  |\\n|--------------------|---------------|-------------|---------|\\n| SmolVLA (base)     | 94.8          | 62.3        | —       |\\n| ACR-VLA            | 92.6          | 70.5        | +8.2    |\\n| SRPO               | 95.6          | 82.1        | +19.8   |\\n| SRPO + ACR-VLA     | 93.1          | 85.0        | +22.7   |\\n| Stacking gain      | -2.5          | +2.9        |         |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The 2.9% stacking gain survives the kill criterion (>2%) but barely. The paper should frame this cautiously: \\\"ACR-VLA provides orthogonal value even atop training-time optimization, though the margin is modest — consistent with SRPO having already resolved many of the uncertainty patterns that ACR-VLA targets.\\\" The 85.0% SOTA claim is legitimate but should acknowledge the narrow margin. Nominal drop of 2.5% on SRPO is slightly higher than on base VLA, possibly because SRPO's actions are more precise and CRM's residuals are more likely to degrade already-good actions.\\n\\n\\nSRPO's training-time optimization partially collapses the flow-matching sample diversity — the model is more deterministic post-finetuning, reducing the information content of K=10 samples. CGS features extracted from SRPO samples have ~22% lower variance compared to base VLA samples, meaning the uncertainty signal is attenuated. CRM still finds residual patterns to correct but the margin is narrower because SRPO has already addressed many of the systematic biases that CRM would otherwise correct. The 2.9% stacking gain (within [2,4]) comes from: (1) dimensions where SRPO's training augmentation didn't cover the specific perturbation pattern (e.g., specific camera angle combinations), and (2) high-uncertainty steps where SRPO's flow-matching still produces diffuse samples and CRM's conformal features provide useful correction signal. The gain is near but above the 2% kill threshold, supporting the claim \\\"complementary in principle, with modest empirical stacking gains.\\\" The 85.0% stacked result (within [84,86]) would be a new LIBERO-Plus SOTA.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"COC Signal Validation\",\n    \"description\": \"Three-part validation of Chunk Overlap Consistency as self-referential ACI update signal: (a) Spearman ρ between episode-level COC coverage fraction and binary task success, (b) ACI alpha_t convergence trajectory over evaluation episodes, (c) Ellipsoid volume comparison between ACI-adaptive and static calibration at matched marginal coverage.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"coc_success_spearman_rho\": 0.38,\n      \"coc_success_spearman_p_value\": 0.003,\n      \"aci_convergence_episodes_to_0p02\": 148,\n      \"aci_target_coverage\": 0.9,\n      \"aci_achieved_coverage_at_convergence\": 0.89,\n      \"aci_overshoot_peak_alpha\": 0.22,\n      \"aci_steady_state_alpha\": 0.11,\n      \"ellipsoid_volume_ratio_adaptive_vs_static\": 0.847,\n      \"ellipsoid_volume_narrowing_pct\": 15.3,\n      \"static_calibration_coverage_achieved\": 0.9,\n      \"adaptive_calibration_coverage_achieved\": 0.89\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 3: COC Signal Quality\",\n        \"content\": \"| Metric                              | Value  |\\n|-------------------------------------|--------|\\n| Spearman ρ (COC ↔ success)          | 0.38   |\\n| p-value                              | 0.003  |\\n| ACI convergence (episodes to ±0.02) | 148    |\\n| ACI achieved coverage               | 0.89   |\\n| Ellipsoid narrowing vs static        | 15.3%  |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"COC is validated as a positive but weak signal. ρ=0.38 is statistically significant (p=0.003) but far from the \\\"reliable\\\" threshold that would make COC a standalone monitoring tool. The paper should position COC as: \\\"a step toward fully self-referential uncertainty monitoring that shows promise but requires further development for deployment-grade reliability.\\\" ACI convergence demonstrates the theoretical guarantee holds in practice, but the slow convergence and overshoot suggest that COC's noisy signal makes online adaptation sluggish. The ellipsoid narrowing result is the strongest evidence for ACI's value — same coverage with tighter sets means more informative uncertainty features for CRM.\\n\\n\\nCOC provides a per-step proxy for whether the model's predictions are self-consistent across overlapping action chunks — high overlap agreement correlates with accurate predictions, low agreement with uncertain/inaccurate ones. However, the correlation with task success is attenuated (ρ=0.38, within [0.3, 0.5]) because: (1) task success is binary while COC is continuous, creating threshold effects; (2) a single bad step can fail a task regardless of high average COC; (3) some tasks succeed despite low COC through robustness of the underlying dynamics. The positive but weak correlation means COC is a \\\"promising\\\" self-referential signal but not \\\"reliable\\\" enough to be the primary ACI driver — episode-level success (when available) would be more informative. ACI converges but slowly (~148 episodes, within expected range for weak signal) with initial overshoot (alpha peaks at 0.22 before settling to 0.11) because COC-based updates have high variance. The 15.3% ellipsoid narrowing (within [10, 20]) demonstrates that adaptive calibration produces tighter prediction sets than static — same coverage guarantee but more informative uncertainty regions. The narrowing is modest because K=10 covariance estimates limit how sharp the ellipsoids can get.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"A1 Ablation — CRM without ACI (Static Calibration)\",\n    \"description\": \"Full CRM with per-DOF conformal features, but CGS thresholds fixed at calibration-set values — no online ACI adaptation. COC is not computed. Tests the marginal contribution of ACI.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate\": 67.8,\n      \"libero_plus_gain_over_base\": 5.5,\n      \"a1_gap_to_full_acr_vla\": 2.7,\n      \"libero_nominal_success_rate\": 93.4,\n      \"static_coverage_at_early_episodes\": 0.93,\n      \"static_coverage_at_late_episodes\": 0.82\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Ablation Table Row (A1)\",\n        \"content\": \"| Variant                | LIBERO-Plus | Δ vs Full |\\n|-----------------------|-------------|-----------|\\n| A1: CRM, static CGS  | 67.8        | -2.7      |\\n| Full ACR-VLA          | 70.5        | —         |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"ACI contributes ~2.7% absolute, which is meaningful but secondary to the conformal features themselves. The coverage drift analysis (0.93→0.82 for static) provides a clean mechanistic explanation for why adaptive calibration helps. This supports Claim 2 but with caveats — ACI is beneficial but not transformative.\\n\\n\\nWithout ACI, conformal thresholds are calibrated on the training distribution and remain fixed. Under LIBERO-Plus perturbations, the calibration set is no longer representative: early evaluation episodes see conservative over-coverage (0.93 vs target 0.90) because perturbations haven't fully diverged from calibration conditions, but later episodes see under-coverage (0.82) as distribution shift accumulates. This miscalibration means CRM receives systematically biased uncertainty features — over-estimated for some perturbation types, under-estimated for others. The 2.7% gap to full ACR-VLA reflects that ACI's online correction produces better-calibrated inputs for CRM, particularly for perturbation dimensions where the shift is gradual and ACI can track it (camera, lighting). For abrupt shifts (layout, language), ACI has less time to adapt, so the gap is smaller there. The result validates ACI's contribution but shows it's not the dominant factor — static conformal features still carry most of the correction signal.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"A2 Ablation — ACI without CRM (Sample Selection Only)\",\n    \"description\": \"CGS + COC + ACI pipeline but instead of CRM, uses multivariate-CP-guided sample selection (extend ReconVLA's approach to leverage per-DOF conformal radii for selecting best sample from K=10). No learned correction, just informed selection.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate\": 66.9,\n      \"libero_plus_gain_over_base\": 4.6,\n      \"a2_gap_to_full_acr_vla\": 3.6,\n      \"libero_nominal_success_rate\": 94.2\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Ablation Table Row (A2)\",\n        \"content\": \"| Variant               | LIBERO-Plus | LIBERO (nom.) | Δ Plus vs Full |\\n|----------------------|-------------|---------------|----------------|\\n| A2: Selection only   | 66.9        | 94.2          | -3.6           |\\n| Full ACR-VLA         | 70.5        | 92.6          | —              |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"A2 provides a useful reference point — it validates that CGS features enable better sample selection (extending ReconVLA), and quantifies CRM's value as a learned corrector (+3.6%). The nominal preservation advantage of A2 is worth discussing as a practical deployment consideration: if nominal drop must be minimized, selection-only is safer.\\n\\n\\nSample selection with multivariate CP features is a strict improvement over ReconVLA's scalar selection — per-DOF conformal radii enable selecting the sample that is best specifically in the most-uncertain dimensions, rather than best in aggregate Euclidean distance. The 4.6% gain over base is substantial and validates that CGS features contain useful information. However, selection is fundamentally limited to choosing among the K=10 samples — it cannot synthesize an action better than any individual sample, which is CRM's advantage. The 3.6% gap to full ACR-VLA quantifies CRM's contribution as a learned synthesizer. Notably, the 0.6% nominal drop is much smaller than full ACR-VLA's 2.2% — because sample selection is conservative (it only picks from generated samples, never modifying them), it's less likely to degrade in-distribution performance. This creates an interesting trade-off for the paper to discuss.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Per-Perturbation-Dimension Breakdown\",\n    \"description\": \"Detailed ACR-VLA gain analysis across 7 LIBERO-Plus perturbation dimensions, evaluated per-task (10 tasks × 7 dimensions = 70 cells). Statistical significance via paired bootstrap (1000 resamples, α=0.05) over 5 seeds.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"camera_shift_gain\": 12.6,\n      \"camera_shift_p_value\": 0.001,\n      \"sensor_noise_gain\": 10.8,\n      \"sensor_noise_p_value\": 0.002,\n      \"robot_state_gain\": 8.5,\n      \"robot_state_p_value\": 0.008,\n      \"lighting_gain\": 5.8,\n      \"lighting_p_value\": 0.031,\n      \"background_gain\": 3.2,\n      \"background_p_value\": 0.112,\n      \"layout_gain\": 0.9,\n      \"layout_p_value\": 0.483,\n      \"language_gain\": 0.9,\n      \"language_p_value\": 0.501,\n      \"dims_with_significant_gain\": 4,\n      \"dims_significant_list\": \"camera_shift, sensor_noise, robot_state, lighting\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 4: Per-Perturbation ACR-VLA Gains\",\n        \"content\": \"| Perturbation   | Base  | ACR-VLA | Δ     | p-value | Sig.? |\\n|---------------|-------|---------|-------|---------|-------|\\n| camera_shift  | 58.2  | 70.8    | +12.6 | 0.001   | ✓     |\\n| sensor_noise  | 55.6  | 66.4    | +10.8 | 0.002   | ✓     |\\n| robot_state   | 56.8  | 65.3    | +8.5  | 0.008   | ✓     |\\n| lighting      | 65.4  | 71.2    | +5.8  | 0.031   | ✓     |\\n| background    | 68.3  | 71.5    | +3.2  | 0.112   | ✗     |\\n| layout        | 72.1  | 73.0    | +0.9  | 0.483   | ✗     |\\n| language      | 71.5  | 72.4    | +0.9  | 0.501   | ✗     |\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 4b: Per-Task × Per-Perturbation Heatmap Data (top-5 tasks by gain)\",\n        \"content\": \"| Task              | camera | noise | robot | light | bg   | layout | lang |\\n|-------------------|--------|-------|-------|-------|------|--------|------|\\n| pick_place_can    | +18.0  | +14.0 | +12.0 | +8.0  | +4.0 | +2.0   | +0.0 |\\n| open_drawer       | +16.0  | +12.0 | +10.0 | +6.0  | +4.0 | +0.0   | +2.0 |\\n| stack_blocks      | +14.0  | +14.0 | +10.0 | +8.0  | +2.0 | +0.0   | +0.0 |\\n| close_microwave   | +12.0  | +10.0 | +8.0  | +6.0  | +4.0 | +2.0   | +0.0 |\\n| turn_faucet       | +10.0  | +8.0  | +6.0  | +4.0  | +2.0 | +0.0   | +2.0 |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"4/7 dimensions with significant gain is within [3, 5]. This result is central to the median-tier narrative: ACR-VLA works well where calibration coverage exists, honestly fails where it doesn't. The paper should present this transparency as a strength — \\\"we clearly identify the boundary of our method's applicability and provide a mechanistic explanation.\\\" The correlation between augmentation coverage and gain (r=0.91) is a strong explanatory result that reviewers will appreciate. Layout/language as honest limitations, with ACI online adaptation as partial mitigation and future work on semantic augmentation as natural extension.\\n\\n\\nThe per-perturbation structure directly reflects synthetic augmentation coverage. Camera jitter, Gaussian sensor noise, and robot-state perturbation are all covered by synthetic augmentation — CGS sees similar distribution shifts during calibration, producing well-calibrated conformal features that CRM can exploit. Camera shift shows the largest gain (+12.6%) because it primarily affects translational DOFs (x, y) where per-DOF conformal features are most discriminative — camera shift makes position uncertain while orientation remains relatively stable, and per-DOF features capture this asymmetry. Lighting (+5.8%) reaches significance because brightness changes create predictable patterns in flow-matching sample variance. Background changes (+3.2%) cause modest, diffuse uncertainty increase that CRM partially addresses, but the effect is not statistically significant at α=0.05. Layout (+0.9%) and language (+0.9%) are essentially unaddressed because these are semantic shifts that synthetic augmentation cannot cover — the calibration set contains no examples of rearranged furniture or paraphrased instructions, so CGS features under these perturbations are poorly calibrated and CRM cannot extract useful correction signal.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"A5 Ablation — CGS Joint Mahalanobis vs Per-DOF Bonferroni\",\n    \"description\": \"Compare full CGS (joint Mahalanobis with Ledoit-Wolf shrunk covariance) vs per-DOF independent Bonferroni-corrected intervals. Tests whether multivariate CP's joint structure provides value beyond simply having per-DOF information.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"joint_mahalanobis_libero_plus\": 70.5,\n      \"bonferroni_libero_plus\": 69.1,\n      \"gap_joint_vs_bonferroni\": 1.4,\n      \"bonferroni_coverage\": 0.94,\n      \"joint_coverage\": 0.9,\n      \"bonferroni_avg_ellipsoid_volume\": 1.0,\n      \"joint_avg_ellipsoid_volume\": 0.72\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"A5 Ablation Results\",\n        \"content\": \"| CGS Variant         | LIBERO-Plus | Coverage | Avg. Volume |\\n|--------------------|-------------|----------|-------------|\\n| Joint Mahalanobis  | 70.5        | 0.90     | 0.72 (norm) |\\n| Per-DOF Bonferroni | 69.1        | 0.94     | 1.00 (norm) |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The 1.4% gap is small but validates that joint multivariate CP provides value through covariance-aware regions. Worth reporting as a supplementary ablation to support the multivariate CP design choice. The over-coverage of Bonferroni (0.94 vs target 0.90) is an inherent inefficiency that the joint approach avoids.\\n\\n\\nBonferroni correction treats each DOF independently and inflates thresholds to maintain joint coverage — producing conservative, hypercubic confidence regions that ignore cross-DOF covariance. Joint Mahalanobis with shrunk covariance produces ellipsoidal regions aligned with the actual covariance structure, yielding tighter (28% smaller volume) regions at the same coverage level. The 1.4% performance gap comes from CRM receiving more informative features: joint CGS features encode which DOF pairs co-vary (e.g., x and y under camera shift), enabling CRM to make correlated corrections across related DOFs. With Bonferroni, CRM sees each DOF's uncertainty independently and must re-learn any cross-DOF correction patterns from the observation embedding alone. The gap is modest because K=10 Ledoit-Wolf covariance is noisy — the theoretical advantage of joint Mahalanobis is attenuated by covariance estimation error, and Bonferroni's conservative margins partially compensate by providing wider \\\"safety padding.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"A6 Ablation — COC Signal vs Episode-Level Success for ACI\",\n    \"description\": \"Compare ACI driven by COC (self-referential, per-step) vs ACI driven by episode-level binary task success (stronger signal, but requires ground-truth outcome and only updates once per episode).\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"coc_based_aci_libero_plus\": 70.5,\n      \"episode_based_aci_libero_plus\": 71.8,\n      \"gap_episode_vs_coc\": 1.3,\n      \"coc_aci_convergence_episodes\": 148,\n      \"episode_aci_convergence_episodes\": 42,\n      \"coc_aci_coverage_variance\": 0.018,\n      \"episode_aci_coverage_variance\": 0.006\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"A6 Ablation: ACI Signal Comparison\",\n        \"content\": \"| ACI Signal     | LIBERO-Plus | Convergence (eps) | Coverage Var. |\\n|---------------|-------------|-------------------|---------------|\\n| COC (self-ref)| 70.5        | 148               | 0.018         |\\n| Episode success| 71.8       | 42                | 0.006         |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Episode-based ACI is clearly superior when ground-truth is available. COC's value proposition is self-referentiality for deployment scenarios without outcome feedback. The 1.3% gap and 3.5x convergence slowdown are honest costs of self-referentiality. Paper recommendation: use episode-level ACI as the default in simulation experiments; present COC as a deployment-oriented variant with known performance trade-offs. This supports repositioning COC as \\\"promising future work\\\" rather than the primary ACI signal.\\n\\n\\nEpisode-level success is a stronger, lower-variance signal for ACI — it directly reflects whether the uncertainty calibration led to good outcomes. COC is noisy because chunk overlap consistency is only a proxy for prediction quality, and individual step-level COC values have high variance. The 1.3% gap reflects that episode-based ACI converges ~3.5x faster (42 vs 148 episodes) and maintains tighter coverage tracking (variance 0.006 vs 0.018). However, episode-level success requires ground-truth task outcomes — available in simulation but not in real deployment. This is the key argument for COC: it's self-referential (no external supervision). The paper should present this as a trade-off: \\\"episode-level ACI is preferable when outcomes are available; COC-based ACI sacrifices ~1.3% performance for full self-referentiality.\\\" In the median tier, COC's disadvantage is noticeable but not disqualifying — it still converges and provides meaningful adaptation, just more slowly and with more variance.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"A7 Ablation — DiU Aleatoric vs CGS Epistemic Features\",\n    \"description\": \"Replace CGS epistemic uncertainty features (45D) with DiU-style aleatoric uncertainty features (7D per-DOF variance from single forward pass). Also test combined epistemic+aleatoric (52D). Validates that epistemic (distribution-shift-sensitive) features are more useful than aleatoric (in-distribution-also-high) features for OOD correction.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"cgs_epistemic_libero_plus\": 70.5,\n      \"diu_aleatoric_libero_plus\": 67.4,\n      \"combined_52d_libero_plus\": 71.1,\n      \"gap_epistemic_vs_aleatoric\": 3.1,\n      \"cgs_epistemic_libero_nominal\": 92.6,\n      \"diu_aleatoric_libero_nominal\": 93.9,\n      \"combined_52d_libero_nominal\": 92.4,\n      \"diu_aleatoric_in_dist_variance\": 0.048,\n      \"cgs_epistemic_in_dist_variance\": 0.012,\n      \"diu_aleatoric_ood_variance\": 0.063,\n      \"cgs_epistemic_ood_variance\": 0.089\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"A7 Ablation: Feature Type Comparison\",\n        \"content\": \"| Feature Type        | Dim | LIBERO (nom.) | LIBERO-Plus | Δ Plus vs Base |\\n|--------------------|-----|---------------|-------------|----------------|\\n| CGS epistemic       | 45  | 92.6          | 70.5        | +8.2           |\\n| DiU aleatoric       | 7   | 93.9          | 67.4        | +5.1           |\\n| Combined            | 52  | 92.4          | 71.1        | +8.8           |\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"A7: Signal Discrimination\",\n        \"content\": \"| Feature Source | In-Dist Var. | OOD Var. | Ratio (OOD/ID) |\\n|--------------|-------------|---------|----------------|\\n| CGS epistemic | 0.012       | 0.089   | 7.42           |\\n| DiU aleatoric | 0.048       | 0.063   | 1.31           |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The 7.42x OOD/ID ratio for epistemic features vs 1.31x for aleatoric validates the core design choice. This is one of the cleaner results — it mechanistically explains why flow-matching sample diversity (epistemic) is more informative than single-pass variance (aleatoric) for distribution shift detection. The combined 52D result (+0.6% over epistemic alone) suggests that adding aleatoric features is marginally beneficial — worth mentioning but not worth the complexity for most applications.\\n\\n\\nDiU captures aleatoric uncertainty — inherent action ambiguity that exists even in-distribution. Under distribution shift, DiU's signal increases only modestly (+31%, 0.048→0.063) because most of what DiU measures is irreducible noise. CGS epistemic features, in contrast, are low in-distribution (0.012) and spike dramatically under shift (+642%, 0.012→0.089), providing a clear signal for \\\"this is out-of-distribution.\\\" CRM conditioned on epistemic features can discriminate OOD steps that need correction from in-distribution steps that don't. With aleatoric features, CRM sees consistently elevated uncertainty everywhere, reducing its ability to be selective. The 3.1% gap validates the epistemic-vs-aleatoric distinction. The combined 52D features provide a marginal +0.6% over epistemic alone — aleatoric features add some complementary information about inherent action ambiguity (useful for multi-modal tasks like placing objects with rotational symmetry), but the gain is small because most of the relevant signal is already captured by epistemic features. The lower nominal drop of DiU (0.9% vs 2.2% for CGS) reflects that aleatoric features are less \\\"aggressive\\\" — they don't provoke large corrections because their signal doesn't differentiate ID from OOD.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Feature Importance Analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Gradient-based feature importance: compute |∂correction/∂feature| averaged over evaluation episodes, broken down by perturbation type. Identifies which of the 45 CGS features drive CRM's corrections for each perturbation dimension.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"top_features_camera_shift\": \"per-DOF std (x, y) — 34% of total gradient; conformal radius (x, y) — 22%\",\n      \"top_features_sensor_noise\": \"per-DOF std (all DOFs) — 41%; ellipsoid volume — 18%\",\n      \"top_features_robot_state\": \"conformal radius (joint 1-4) — 38%; per-DOF std (joint 1-4) — 25%\",\n      \"top_features_lighting\": \"per-DOF std (x, y, z) — 29%; ellipsoid volume — 21%\",\n      \"per_dof_std_importance_share\": 0.42,\n      \"conformal_radius_importance_share\": 0.31,\n      \"ellipsoid_volume_importance_share\": 0.14,\n      \"cross_dof_covariance_importance_share\": 0.13\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 5: Feature Importance by Category\",\n        \"content\": \"| Feature Category    | Importance Share | Primary Perturbations |\\n|--------------------|-----------------|----------------------|\\n| Per-DOF std (7D)   | 42%             | noise, camera, light |\\n| Conformal radius (7D)| 31%           | robot-state, camera  |\\n| Ellipsoid volume (1D)| 14%           | all (gating)         |\\n| Cross-DOF cov (30D) | 13%            | robot-state          |\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The feature importance analysis validates the dimension-selective correction mechanism: CRM learns to attend to different uncertainty features for different perturbation types, without explicit supervision about which perturbation is active. This is a compelling interpretability result for the paper. The 42%+31% = 73% importance of per-DOF features (std + conformal radius) supports Claim 1's emphasis on per-DOF structure, even though the A4 ablation showed only +2.1% over scalar — the mechanism is clearly there, even if the aggregate performance gain is modest.\\n\\n\\nPer-DOF std features are the most important (42% of gradient magnitude) because they directly encode \\\"how uncertain is this specific DOF\\\" — the most actionable information for dimension-selective correction. Conformal radius features rank second (31%) because they encode calibrated confidence intervals that are more robust than raw std to distributional assumptions. Ellipsoid volume (14%) is a global uncertainty summary — useful for CRM's gating mechanism (how much total correction to apply) but less informative for dimension selection. Cross-DOF covariance features (13%) capture correlation structure; their moderate importance reflects that K=10 covariance estimates are noisy, limiting the information content of off-diagonal terms. The perturbation-specific patterns are illuminating: camera shift activates translational DOF features (x, y) because it primarily affects workspace position estimation; robot-state perturbation activates joint-specific conformal radii because it directly perturbs joint configurations; sensor noise activates broad std features because it diffusely increases all DOF uncertainties.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"SnapFlow Integration (K=10 × 1-step)\",\n    \"description\": \"Replace K=10 full denoising trajectories with K=10 single-step distilled generation via SnapFlow-style consistency distillation. Test: (a) inference speedup, (b) sample diversity preservation, (c) downstream ACR-VLA performance.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"full_denoising_inference_ms\": 118,\n      \"snapflow_1step_inference_ms\": 38,\n      \"speedup_factor\": 3.1,\n      \"diversity_trace_ratio\": 0.83,\n      \"full_denoising_libero_plus\": 70.5,\n      \"snapflow_libero_plus\": 69.8,\n      \"performance_drop\": 0.7,\n      \"snapflow_covariance_frobenius_error\": 0.21\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S1: SnapFlow Integration\",\n        \"content\": \"| Sampling Method     | K=10 Time (ms) | Diversity | LIBERO-Plus | Δ   |\\n|--------------------|----------------|-----------|-------------|-----|\\n| Full denoising     | 118            | 1.00      | 70.5        | —   |\\n| SnapFlow 1-step    | 38             | 0.83      | 69.8        | -0.7|\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"SnapFlow integration is a practical engineering contribution. The 0.7% drop is small enough that the 3.1x speedup is clearly worthwhile for deployment. The diversity reduction is the primary concern — if future work increases K beyond 10, SnapFlow's diversity loss may compound. Report as supplementary result demonstrating deployment feasibility.\\n\\nSingle-step distilled generation produces samples ~3.1x faster but with reduced diversity: the trace ratio of 0.83 means 1-step samples capture 83% of the covariance structure of full denoising samples. The 17% diversity loss comes from the distillation process collapsing minor modes in the action distribution — the dominant mode is well-preserved but tail samples are pulled inward. This propagates to a modest 0.7% ACR-VLA performance drop because the CGS features computed from less-diverse samples have slightly attenuated covariance structure, reducing the per-DOF signal quality. The Frobenius error of 0.21 (normalized) in covariance estimation means the Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage target absorbs more weight, further compressing the estimated covariance toward the identity-like shrinkage target. The practical trade-off is favorable: 3.1x speedup for 0.7% accuracy cost, making real-time deployment more feasible. This is a supplementary result that strengthens the practical narrative without affecting claims.\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":326,"startLine":1,"totalLines":326}}}
